{"nodes": [{"id": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_question", "label": "Q16: Data consistency across microservices", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/questions/16-eventual-consistency.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_eventual_consistency", "label": "Eventual consistency", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/questions/16-eventual-consistency.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Read-your-own-writes only holds by reading from the owning service, and monotonic reads need sticky routing or version checks. Being able to say what you cannot promise is worth more than claiming consistency is solved."}, {"id": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_transactional_outbox", "label": "Transactional outbox", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/questions/16-eventual-consistency.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "outbox_messages lives in the service's own database so the row and the message commit in one local transaction; a background publisher drains it. Order by an auto-assigned sequence not a timestamp; a failed publish stops the batch so a poison message cannot let later events overtake it; the message id is assigned once at enqueue time, which is what makes consumer-side deduplication possible."}, {"id": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_dual_write_problem", "label": "The dual-write problem", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/questions/16-eventual-consistency.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Save-then-publish loses the event if the process dies in between; publish-then-save fires an event for a project that was never saved. There is no ordering of two independent writes that is safe."}, {"id": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_make_the_window_visible", "label": "Make the staleness window visible", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/questions/16-eventual-consistency.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A SignalR push and an 'updated Ns ago' column beat arguing: 'accurate as of two seconds ago' is a statement people accept, a silently wrong number is not. Design so the window is safe to observe \u2014 the project sits in an explicit Initiating stage rather than pretending to be Active."}, {"id": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_monitor_queue_depth", "label": "Monitor queue depth, not just errors", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/questions/16-eventual-consistency.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A consumer down for hours is fine \u2014 messages queue and are processed on recovery \u2014 but a queue quietly growing at 3am is much harder to notice than a 500, and exceeding broker retention means lost messages, which is when the rebuild endpoint stops being a nicety."}], "edges": [{"source": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_eventual_consistency", "target": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_question", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/questions/16-eventual-consistency.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_transactional_outbox", "target": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_question", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/questions/16-eventual-consistency.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_dual_write_problem", "target": "questions_16_eventual_consistency_transactional_outbox", "relation": "ratio